! Supplementary NASA-9 records for the open-air thermal route (kerotakis_cea).
!
! SOURCE: R. A. Robie and B. S. Hemingway (1995), Thermodynamic properties of
! minerals and related substances at 298.15 K and 1 bar (10^5 pascals) pressure
! and at higher temperatures, U.S. Geological Survey Bulletin 2131 -- a U.S.
! Government work in the public domain (https://pubs.usgs.gov/bul/2131/report.pdf,
! read 2026-11-20). Used: the Cp(T) polynomial Cp = A1 + A2 T + A3 T^-2 +
! A4 T^-0.5 + A5 T^2 from the table of coefficients, S(298.15) and dfH(298.15).
!   ZINCITE ZnO        A = 43.50, 7.658E-03, -7.573E+05, 54.56, 0     298-1800 K
!                      S = 43.20 J/(mol K), dfH = -350.5 kJ/mol
!   SMITHSONITE ZnCO3  A = 148.38, 2.835E-02, 4.796E+05, -1419, 0    298-1200 K
!                      S = 81.19 J/(mol K), dfH = -817.0 kJ/mol
!
! METHOD (Kerotakis): the T^-0.5 term has no NASA-9 counterpart, so the seven
! NASA-9 Cp coefficients are a least-squares fit to Cp/R of the source
! polynomial on 600 points over the source's own range (R = 8.31446261815324);
! b1 and b2 then make H(298.15) = dfH and S(298.15) = S exactly. Worst relative
! Cp deviation from the source polynomial: 4.7e-6 (ZnO), 1.1e-5 (ZnCO3). Checked
! against the source's printed tables at 298.15/400/600/1000/1400/1800 K (ZnO)
! and 298.15/400/600/800/1000/1200 K (ZnCO3): Cp, S and (H-H298)/T agree to the
! printed 0.01 J/(mol K). tools/robie_hemingway_cea_fit.py regenerates the
! records below (--check verifies them); nothing here is anything but that
! transform of the source.
!
! NOT here: malachite. The same bulletin tabulates it at 298.15 K only (no Cp(T)),
! so it has no record and its decomposition is not computed.
thermo
    200.00   1000.00   6000.00  20000.   9/09/04
ZnO(cr)           Robie & Hemingway 1995 USGS Bull 2131 Cp(T),S,dfH; NASA-9 fit (Kerotakis)
